# KOMHunter Project Rules

## Project Overview
KOMHunter is a Strava segment discovery application that helps athletes find and analyze segments to conquer KOMs (King of the Mountain).

## Architecture
- **Backend**: FastAPI (Python 3.11+) with Pydantic v2
- **Frontend**: Next.js 14+ with TypeScript and TailwindCSS
- **Auth**: OAuth2 with Strava, JWT sessions
- **Map**: Leaflet or Mapbox GL

## Code Standards

### Python (Backend)
- Use type hints everywhere
- Use Pydantic models for all API schemas
- Follow PEP 8 style guide
- Use async/await for I/O operations
- Handle errors with proper HTTP status codes
- Document all endpoints with OpenAPI docstrings

### TypeScript (Frontend)
- Use strict TypeScript (`"strict": true`)
- Define interfaces for all API responses
- Use React Server Components where appropriate
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Use custom hooks for data fetching

### Security
- Never commit secrets or API keys
- Use environment variables for all credentials
- Validate all user inputs
- Implement rate limiting on API endpoints
- Use HTTPS in production

### Testing
- Write unit tests for all services
- Use pytest for backend tests
- Use mocks for external API calls (Strava, Nominatim)
- Maintain 80%+ code coverage
- Test error handling paths

## File Structure

### Backend
```
backend/app/
├── api/routes/     # FastAPI route handlers
├── services/       # Business logic
├── models/         # Pydantic schemas
└── utils/          # Helper functions
```

### Frontend
```
frontend/src/
├── app/           # Next.js app router
├── components/    # React components
├── hooks/         # Custom React hooks
└── lib/           # Utilities and API client
```

## Naming Conventions
- Python: snake_case for functions/variables, PascalCase for classes
- TypeScript: camelCase for functions/variables, PascalCase for types/interfaces
- Files: kebab-case for components, snake_case for Python modules
- API routes: kebab-case (e.g., /api/segments/explore)

## Dependencies
- Always specify exact versions in requirements.txt
- Use npm for frontend package management
- Prefer well-maintained libraries with active communities

## Documentation
- Add docstrings to all Python functions
- Add JSDoc comments to complex TypeScript functions
- Keep README.md updated with setup instructions
- Generate OpenAPI spec at /docs endpoint
